7-8/9/95  Visits to Sabanözü and Avsar Villages -

After finding out that it would cost 1-2 million TL to hire a taxi to go 30 km. to get as far as Avsar, stopping at Sabanözü on the way, I decided to take a dolmus from Polatlı to Sabanözü (SÖ). There are several everyday to that village. The Muhtar arranged for me to stay with the head of the SÖ cooperative, Hüseyin & Behiye Öztoprak. 
I arrived at ca. 5:00 pm on a cloudy day, with a busful of mostly women chatting away, showing their various goods which they bought at the Polatlı market that day...They gave me a choice seat after finding out where I would be staying as a guest.. The Muhtar’s wife has at least 20 gold bracelets....
From a distance, the village at a higher elevation than YH, is entered through a paved road which is lined on both sides with grown poplars. A very green, dramatic entrance. The village is widely distributed along hills and valleys...not a localized, densely located village appearance.
	The general appearance of the village is one of neglect and abandoned houses; highland village, in the valley with a backdrop of Çile Mtns.
House building materials are lots of stone, some mudbrick. Some of the abandoned houses are very substantial, two-storeys.  

Important difference: The first storey is where the animals are kept, storage of foodstuffs and tandır house-- unlike in YH and other villages near it where houses are one storey and  small courtyards where the storage and animal pens are kept. None of houses have ovens..bazlama, gözleme made in tandır,
no somun made.

	I photographed the remains of an abandoned, two-storey house. On the second-storey there is a small fire-place, shape is “kıble”; they were used only for open fire cooking and heating the room. Such installations within the house proper not found in other villages I studied. They have “tandır” houses, as others, but they do not have ovens in the courtyard..or anywhere else. ..that is interesting. 
	Tarhana Making - in the courtyard...

In this village there are a lot of fruit trees, groves, apple, pear, also poplar which is used as building material...stony areas given to grazing.
Crops: cereals, melons, onion, sugar beet..same growing season.. no different processing of the soil than other villages.

General sense of discontent and abandonment of the village. Labor is very short, because younger people leave which directly affects farming and herding. Children make their home elsewhere. When parents die, land is divided among children which is eventually sold or left barren, because such a small plot is not sufficient or economical.
	All surrounding hills are bare, used to have oak and pine..due to cutting and grazing.
	
	At the engagement party, women gathered in the courtyard of the girl, live music, video camera, women started to folk dance. Soon, the bride and groom were taken from her grandparents house by close female relatives and brought to sit on chairs, facing the women...(The men were in a far corner of the courtyard.. they were dancing and chatting among themselves..separation of sexes is so very much alive still, in these parts of the country).
A relative from the girl’s side was selected to show off all the presents which were given to the girl, one by one...she would call out the kind of present, who it was given by and a joke to go along with it...This lasted for a couple of hours, under the intense sun, while the couple was sweating, especially the bride who was heavily made up with blue mascara and purplish red lipstick. Her purple taffeta dress, beaded in front, was full and beautiful when it swirled about during her dance...after the many gold bangles, coins which were put on her dress, money pinned on him and her, the rest of the gifts which were mainly personal dress, towel, lingerie etc... were put gathered on a large metal tray (doubles as a table on floor, “sini”), the guests were given peanuts...the backdrop to this celebration were the slopes of Çile Mtns....
Tomorrow is another celebration at the boy’s house for men, while the women go to the girls house...The fourth day is Sunday, the girl is brought to the boy’s house, in the afternoon, among much aplomb and singing and dancing the guests from both sides are served food and sweets, finally the two stay at the house in the same courtyard as the boys parents.. A new life begins...(The men’s gatherings usually have women dancers brought in from outside and much drinking and very loud music which the whole village has to endure throughout the night.)

Hüseyin Öztoprak - member of committee for the Irrigation Project

Hüseyin Öztoprak - 
	
	Elevation is ca. 900 m. here. Has lots of drinking water, which comes from different springs, from Çile Mtns. Pop. is ca. 1,600-1700, 300 HH. So it is ca. 4 times bigger than YH; largest village in region of Polatlı.  But no irrigation, because Sakarya is far away....and underground water is too far down, so they cannot get it out to irrigate....only a few fields near Sakarya can be irrigated. 
Relationship between AV and SÖ used to be quite lively, going back to 40-45 years ago. Behiye said they would take milk products, wool on donkeys and horses to their friends at AR and exchange them for chick peas, lentils.
STRATEGY : (This is an interesting exchange which has archaeological implications-- that is, an explanation for basis of subsistence-- by exchange between nearby villages which are not even ethnically related--
SÖ village was originally Greek (he calls them Armenian!). This is the largest village of Polatlı and is reputedly the oldest. Now the population is made up of people from Ayas, Haymana, from the east, Beypazarı, Sivrihisar (noone from Kıbrıscık). 
Total planted land area is 20,000 d; mera is 10,000 d; village and gardens, 
ca. 1,000 d. So total is ca: 31,000 d. Even though the land area is bigger than YH,
land per HH is much less.. .103 d./HH (SÖ); 275 d./HH (YH).
Also, lack of proximity to Sakarya is a great disadvantage. 
Fields are as far as Cekirdeksiz territory (10-12 km); Sakarya is 10 km; Müslim village, 15 km. 
Where were the earliest fields located? In 1900 to 1920’s there were 60 HH. 
Fields were very close to the center. Was there water there? Was the soil good? Most people chose closer territory because they did not have good horses or oxen for carrying tools and men for the ploughing. Transportation was difficult. So, technology, not water, was the limiting factor in the selection of fields. Because water was only available from Hüyük spring, Akpınar suyu, which came from the mountains...used it 60 years ago..but now they are dry. 
They did not establish the village near the springs to avoid mosquitoes; they went uphill and here also they found good drinking water near the village.
With increase in population,  farther plots were ploughed; then, transition from oxen to horses to tractor in early 1950’s....most of the grazing land was converted to farming...
DRY FARMING: Since 10 yrs. there is almost no irrigation, very few well water used..We are 10 km. from Sakarya. There are only 2-3 HH that have fields near Sakarya. So, all farming is mainly dry farming...And, crops are wheat and barley. Only some sugar beets and onions watered by wells. In the past, we used to plant navy beans, lentils, chick peas, melon...But then there was more rain, up until 20 years ago.. we used to make canals from Hüyük spring. Then we had ca. 2,000 d. of sugar beets. Now, only 200 d. sugar beets..

ANIMAL HUSBANDRY: Up until 40-50 yrs. ago there was 10,000 sheep & goats, 2,000 cows. Now, it is 4,000 sheep & goats, 1,000 cows. 
Feed is the critical factor. Because not enough extra cash to buy it, since irrigation for cash crops is absent, so cash crops not sufficiently profitable.
In the absence of water, why not raise more animals? But we have to buy the feed still, if we cannot raise enough barley and fodder...In addition, the villager has become very lazy. (I think, they don’t calculate the optimum yield from animals too well!). In most of the surrounding villages, only 20-25% of the people keep animals.
Seasonality: In spring, sheep that do not give milk go to Yayla, Kozdere ve Kızılarkaç, ca. 5 km. from here. There is water and good grasses there. After 3 months, they come down (July-Aug.) to cereal fields, but still does not come to the village till winter. The rest graze daily, come to the village for milking at night. So, there is more potential at SÖ for herding....
(Funda= clover)

Another important factor in the decrease of herding is the lack of LABOR and high shepherd wages. 
It is profitable to do herding, even without farming cereals....

How did your father farm? How much land did he have ? We were 7.
During my father’s time, we had ca. 500-600 d.; now we still have the same.
But the population of the family has increased 10-fold. We divided it, now each has less than 100 d. And, I have 7 children-- how can I divided that into 7? It will end up being 15 d. /child...We could not increase it from my father’s time...because there is now a lot of consumers goods to buy.
Your father did dry farming of cereals and lentils, chick peas etc.....In order to counteract the risks, what did they do? Then, it rained a lot, 50 yrs. ago. much more snow....we didn’t even put fertilizer...also, earth was more fertile, now it is tired. The fallow now is not really nadas....we plant sunflowers, chickpeas in the fallow fields. But we do not get as good yield. The fertilizer, in the lack of rain burns the crop. So, even crop rotation is not very effective.

Fifty years ago the pop. here was 300-400 people (1/4 of today). 
Water from here goes to Sazılar and Kıranharman and Beylikköprü...so it is being used for drinking water and we don’t have enough for irrigation.


